\begin_layout Itemize
At the Redhat Gnome Login enter your dcs account details.
 The machine will then enter Linux with the Gnome desktop.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Right click on the desktop and select 
\begin_inset LatexCommand \url{New > Terminal}

\end_inset

.
 A `terminal' window will then be opened for you in which you can write
 command line arguments.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Create a new directory called npalab in your home directory by writing 
\begin_inset LatexCommand \url{cd}

\end_inset

pressing return and then writing 
\begin_inset LatexCommand \url{mkdir}

\end_inset

\InsetSpace ~

\begin_inset LatexCommand \url{npalab}

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Open a Netscape window using the icon on the desktop and point the browser
 at 
\begin_inset LatexCommand \url{http://www.dcs.shef.ac.uk/~neil/NPA/source.zip}

\end_inset

.
 When the Save As box appears save the file in the directory you have just
 created.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Move to the directory with 
\begin_inset LatexCommand \url{cd}

\end_inset

\InsetSpace ~

\begin_inset LatexCommand \url{npalab}

\end_inset

.
 Uncompress the 
\begin_inset LatexCommand \url{source.zip}

\end_inset

 file with the command 
\begin_inset LatexCommand \url{unzip}

\end_inset

\InsetSpace ~

\begin_inset LatexCommand \url{source.zip}

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate
Open the file 
\begin_inset LatexCommand \url{mm1.tcl}

\end_inset

 by typing 
\begin_inset LatexCommand \url{gedit}

\end_inset

\InsetSpace ~

\begin_inset LatexCommand \url{mm1.tcl}

\end_inset

\InsetSpace ~

\begin_inset LatexCommand \url{&}

\end_inset

 (the & makes the process run in the background).
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Standard
This file contains the simulation of the 
\begin_inset Formula $M/M/1$
\end_inset

 queue.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Look at the top part of the file.
 Here the arrival rate is set, 
\begin_inset LatexCommand \url{lambda}

\end_inset

.
 The parameter 
\begin_inset LatexCommand \url{mu}

\end_inset

 controls the exponential distribution for the packet lengths.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Find the part in the file which is commented
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
# Set up the network structure
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
It is here that the network topology is set out.
 Find and read the parts where the different nodes are created.
 Also study the line which creates the link between the nodes.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
set link [$ns simplex-link $node1 $node2 8Kb 0ms DropTail]
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
What do you think the different parts of this line do? What does simplex
 mean?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Try running the simulation by typing 
\begin_inset LatexCommand \url{ns}

\end_inset

\InsetSpace ~

\begin_inset LatexCommand \url{mm1.tcl}

\end_inset

.
 The queue length will be output to the sreen.
 You can put the queue length in a file by writing 
\begin_inset LatexCommand \url{ns}

\end_inset

\InsetSpace ~

\begin_inset LatexCommand \url{mm1.tcl}

\end_inset

\InsetSpace ~

\begin_inset LatexCommand \url{>}

\end_inset

\InsetSpace ~

\begin_inset LatexCommand \url{qlength.dat}

\end_inset

.
 You can study the results of the simulation by studing them in MATLAB.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Start MATLAB with 
\begin_inset LatexCommand \url{matlab}

\end_inset

\InsetSpace ~

\begin_inset LatexCommand \url{&}

\end_inset

.
 Change to the 
\begin_inset LatexCommand \url{npalab}

\end_inset

 directory and load the file with the command 
\begin_inset LatexCommand \url{load}

\end_inset

\InsetSpace ~

\begin_inset LatexCommand \url{qlength.dat}

\end_inset

.
 You can plot the average over time 
\begin_inset LatexCommand \url{plot(cumsum(qlength)./(1:length(qlength))')}

\end_inset

.
 Additionally you can use MATLAB to check the mean of the data.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Run the simulation for an arrival rate, 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda$
\end_inset

, of 1 and a packet length distribution (in Kb) with a rate of 2.
 Then run the simulation a second time with the same packet length distribution
 and an arrival rate of 1.9.
 Compute the theoretical average queue length in each case and compare with
 your plotted values.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate
Now you will extend the simulation by adding a third node and a further
 link and associated queue.
 The structure of the network you will now create will be as below 
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset VSpace 0.3cm
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\align center
\begin_inset Graphics
	filename diagrams/ns2mm2Tandem.eps
	display color
	width 50text%

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset VSpace 0.3cm
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
First of all
\series default
 use `Save As' to save your current file with the name 
\begin_inset LatexCommand \url{tandemMM1.tcl}

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
After the line
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout LyX-Code
set node2 [$ns node]
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
add a new node, called 
\begin_inset LatexCommand \url{node3}

\end_inset

, by writing
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
set node3 [$ns node]
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
After the line
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout LyX-Code
set link [$ns simplex-link $node1 $node2 8Kb 0ms DropTail]
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
add a new link, connecting the new node, called 
\begin_inset LatexCommand \url{link2}

\end_inset

, by writing:
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
set link2 [$ns simplex-link $node2 $node3 8Kb 0ms DropTail]
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
Now associate a new queue with this link by adding the lines
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout LyX-Code
set qu2 [[$ns link $node2 $node3] set queue_]
\newline
$qu2 set limit_ $m
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
You need to change the node where the packets leave the network from 
\begin_inset LatexCommand \url{node2}

\end_inset

 to 
\begin_inset LatexCommand \url{node3}

\end_inset

.
 Do this by changing the line
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout LyX-Code
$ns attach-agent $node2 $sink
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
to 
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
$ns attach-agent $node3 $sink
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
To monitor the queue you need to add this line
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout LyX-Code
set qmon2 [$ns monitor-queue $node2 $node3 ""]
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
Finally you need to print the new queue length by changing the lines in
 the function 
\begin_inset LatexCommand \url{outputdata}

\end_inset

 
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout LyX-Code
global ns qmon
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
to
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
global ns qmon qmon2
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
and replace the line 
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
puts $NQ
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
with the lines
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
set NQ2 [$qmon2 set pkts_]
\newline
puts -nonewline $NQ
\newline
puts -nonewline ", "
\newline
puts $NQ2
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
You should now be able to plot the results in MATLAB.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\end_deeper
